{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Phase 1 RCA (confirmed before repair): the production snapshot contract deliberately publishes files/directories as 0444/0555 in `src/rebar/llm/workflow/snapshot.py:_chmod_readonly`. On this macOS host, an ephemeral tree with that exact shape caused plain `rm -rf` to fail with `Permission denied` on the file and `Directory not empty` on every ancestor, reproducing the original CI mechanism. Running the existing `snapshot._rmtree_writable` seam against the same residual tree removed it completely, ruling out APFS/RAM-disk specifics and proving permission restoration is sufficient. Current main applies that teardown only through `_remove_readonly_snapshot_after_test` in `tests/unit/workflow/test_snapshot.py`; symbol/caller inspection found no consumer that behaviorally depends on cleanup being module-local. The structural defect is ownership: another snapshot-producing test module can leave the identical non-removable basetemp because shared `tests/conftest.py` does not enforce the cleanup invariant. Defect legitimacy is established by the original three-run macOS failure, closed fix commit `7d908fbbc6`, and the operator requirement to repair the whole cleanup class while preserving read-only behavior. Expected post-fix behavior: snapshots remain non-writable during each test, and every `.rebar/run_snapshots` root under pytest-owned `tmp_path` is made removable during shared teardown; unrelated temp content is untouched."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787028129344012001,"uuid":"dd694c38-83e0-4402-b8bb-7acd78621149"}